What was rewritten: the plugin folder (a plugin.json manifest, an mcp.json naming
one Streamable HTTP endpoint, and a skill) is here a cell with no manifest and no
MCP runtime. The same JSON-RPC 2.0 goes to the same endpoint,
https://api.lovedone.app/mcp/v1, with the same family MCP key in the same
Authorization bearer header; the client half -- initialize, the Mcp-Session-Id
header, notifications/initialized, tools/call, and the event-stream framing the
transport allows -- is written against the image's own HTTP call. The ten tools
its README documents keep their server-side names and arguments and answer the
server's own text; each is one model tool here, prefixed lovedone_, with
lovedone_call as the escape hatch for any tool a later server adds. The skill's
guidance -- senior tech support, not healthcare; confirm before a write; never
paste the key into chat -- is the cell's primer and its tool descriptions. The
key moves from LOVEDONE_API_KEY only into the cell's lovedone section, which
still reads that variable when nothing is pasted there.

What was left out: the trust declaration and the OAuth flow. Hermes was told the
server is untrusted and asked before each write-capable tool call; here the cell
says so in its tool descriptions (WRITES) and its primer asks for confirmation,
but nothing enforces the pause before a tool runs. The original also let the
server complete OAuth 2.1 with PKCE; this cell authenticates with the MCP key
only, which the plugin's own manifest header used. The tool schemas are the
documented set rather than a tools/list fetched at run time, so a tool the
server adds or renames after the reviewed commit is reached through lovedone_call,
and the cell never dials to learn a schema. Nothing else of the plugin is
missing: it had no commands, hooks or prompts of its own beyond the skill.
